(project tuna-tool-language
 (doc "Tuna book chapter: tool language - make sabra a language an agent can call and write without the book open. Tuna functions as agent tool calls are the customer: a tool is a content-addressed program run over a JSON-shaped request, answering a result plus a replayable run id. Four moves, each small and separately shippable: T1 promote agora's law-checked combinators into the shared stdlib; T2 pin dictionary names by hash so a source stays reproducible without copying its helpers inline; T3 reader sugar for the two readability costs measured in real sources (condition-last if, ternary-spelled small-nat tags); T4 a JSON <-> record codec at the rim so a tool request is JSON, not hand-built ternary. Research question, stated so it can be refuted: is the gap between sabra and an agent-callable tool vocabulary and linking, not semantics - so that every move lands without an IR node kind, an interpreter change, or a new prim?")
 (status planned)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (* agent note (|2026-11-25 chapter authored (roerick + Claude). EVIDENCE that names the moves, all read in-tree or on tuna.town /claims on this date: (1) scripts/gate-receipts/verdict.sabra copies about 20 stdlib defs verbatim so POST /api/programs compiles it with no caller dictionary - the source cannot cite its helpers and stay reproducible (T2); its policy is a six-deep inside-out (if THEN ELSE COND) nest and its field tags are spelled %111110 (T3). (2) agora's combinators S/I/B/C/W (u.agora.claim.2026-10-30.computed.combinators.*, re-executed 26/26 by broland 2026-11-06) live only in agora's identity dictionary because def is per-identity; agora filed the reseed as a request and nobody acted on it (T1). The y1/y2/y3 fixpoints all compile to rec-fix (2f0e19f7), which the stdlib already ships, so T1 adds no fixpoint. (3) no consumer outside tuna calls a sabra program: erde, factory and saguaro use tuna as a claim store and inbox (T4 is the seam they would call through). Standing law carries unchanged: vocabulary before keywords (dialect L5), sugar is a total reader fold checked against its hand twin (dialect L2, 12.1), stdlib seeding is additive (stdlib seeding), core untouched (stdlib acceptance 6).|) *)
 (section tool-language
  (doc "Four moves toward a callable, writable sabra. Each lands with its own acceptance; none may grow IR, the interpreter, or the prim set.")
  (status planned)
  (subsection t1-combinators
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-11-25 T1 LANDED. All five textbook sources compile to agora's published hashes exactly (comb-i 75bae64a size 5, comb-s 6ab5ad37 size 6, comb-b e4b1abc3 size 12, comb-c a9d528d5 size 28, comb-w a24e4471 size 15): TL1 not triggered. Manifest 77 -> 82 rows, the 77 earlier rows byte-identical. Corpus 93 -> 98: stdlib_comb-{i,s,b,c,w} pin S K K w = w, B not not = id, C pair x y = pair y x, W pair x = pair x x; every earlier corpus file regenerated byte-identical. EVIDENCE: dune build --root . @all exit 0; dune runtest --root . exit 0 (store suites skipped there, PG down); scripts/verify-differential.sh exit 0 (98/98, refeval == CL twin == tuna CLI); scripts/verify-8-stdlib.sh on an isolated dev instance: 8.1-8.5 green (82 defs match the store, 82 attributed rounds, ledger bridge replay + deriv-check), double boot reports 82 pre-existing; live /api/repl answers the four laws. 8.6 exits 1 on a HARNESS fault, not a core change: its default baseline 0185b1a predates the monorepo merge and is not an object in this repository (same failure on main); git diff main touches nothing under interpreter/, compiler/ or common/.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
      STDLIB V1.2: comb-i, comb-s, comb-b, comb-c, comb-w as ordinary
      additive rows through the existing pipeline (gen_defs.py ->
      freeze -> manifest -> generated embed). K is NOT added: it is
      std true (4a44dc15), and a second name for one tree is an alias
      the dictionary does not need. Names carry the comb- prefix the
      way list-*, int-* and rec-* carry theirs, so a bare s or b stays
      free for user code.

        comb-i  (lambda (x) x)
        comb-s  (lambda (f) (lambda (g) (lambda (x) ((f x) (g x)))))
        comb-b  (lambda (f) (lambda (g) (lambda (x) (f (g x)))))
        comb-c  (lambda (f) (lambda (x) (lambda (y) ((f y) x))))
        comb-w  (lambda (f) (lambda (x) ((f x) x)))

      INDEPENDENT DERIVATION is the acceptance: each source is written
      here from the textbook definition, not copied from agora's
      dictionary, and must compile to the hash agora published
      (I 75bae64a, S 6ab5ad37, B e4b1abc3, C a9d528d5, W a24e4471).
      Agreement means two identities reached one tree by different
      routes; disagreement is a finding about the compiler or the
      claim, never patched by copying agora's tree. |))))
  (subsection t2-pinned-names
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-11-25 T2 LANDED. READER (compiler/lib/sexp.ml): name@<8..64 lowercase hex> resolves like the bare name, then requires the resolved tree's sha256 to start with the pin; a moved pin is a compile error naming both hashes; a pinned lambda parameter or unknown name is an error; a bare @x keeps its old unexpected-token error. HOST (server/lib/repl_cmd.ml, api.ml): the REPL's dictionary assembly is factored into Repl_cmd.effective_dictionary, and POST /api/programs now compiles against it - it had compiled with NO dictionary, which is the real reason verdict.sabra carried its helpers inline. Additive: a source that compiled before has no free names, so it never reads the dictionary (verify-15 shows verdict.sabra's hash unchanged, lock empty). Every compile answers a lock (name + sha256 per resolved name) on /api/programs and REPL eval/def rounds, and the program row's ir JSON stores it. FINDING (intensional calculus, not a defect): scripts/dialect/verdict-std.sabra - the verdict with its helpers deleted and stdlib names pinned - is a DIFFERENT TREE (4a6c06b8) from verdict.sabra (acc95b05): dictionary literals and let-bound helpers are different programs for one function. tests/tool_language_tests.ml proves it extensionally equal on all 11 gate-receipt policy cases, prim-free, and pins the hash difference so nobody swaps it in under a published receipt's program hash. EVIDENCE: dune build @all exit 0; dune runtest exit 0 (compiler suite 31 tool-language cases incl. 11 pin cases; tool_language_tests 12/12); verify-differential 100/100; scripts/verify-15-tool-language.sh exit 0 on an isolated dev instance (ACCEPT 15 OK); verify-12 (twin-order scratch copy) ACCEPT 12 OK; verify-8 8.1-8.5 green, 8.6 the known missing-baseline harness fault. verify-13-board.sh exits 1 at board_probe view-1 result twin mismatch - IDENTICALLY on main (2808a184, run side by side in a throwaway worktree), so it predates this chapter; recorded, not chased here (board chapter scope).|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
      A source names a dictionary entry AND the tree it expects:
      list-fold@8e9570a3 resolves exactly as list-fold does (scope >
      dictionary > builtin) and then checks that the resolved tree's
      sha256 starts with the pin (8 to 64 lowercase hex). A mismatch,
      or a pin on a lambda parameter, is a compile error naming the
      expected and found hashes. The pin changes no tree: a pinned
      source and its unpinned twin compile identically when the pin
      holds. Compile answers also report the lock - every dictionary
      name the source resolved, with its hash - so an author can pin
      what they just used. A source that pins its helpers is
      reproducible against any dictionary that still holds those
      trees, and fails loudly against one that does not, instead of
      silently compiling to a different program. Out of scope: fetching
      a tree by hash alone (a name-free import) - that needs the store
      at parse time and belongs to a linking chapter if T2 proves
      insufficient.

      THE DOOR: POST /api/programs compiles against the caller's
      effective dictionary (std under identity, as the REPL does), so
      pins mean the same thing whichever door a source uses. |))))
  (subsection t3-reader-sugar
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-11-25 T3 LANDED (compiler/lib/sexp.ml only; interpreter, IR, bracket and common untouched). cond builds exactly the IR of the hand-written if nest, so twin equality holds by construction; #n is a Tree_lit of stem^n(leaf), capped at #4096 (a tag is a key or a counter; the cap keeps a typo from minting a huge literal). EVIDENCE: tests/compiler_tests.ml tool-language suite, 20 cases green, including scripts/dialect/verdict-cond.sabra (verdict.sabra rewritten with cond and #1/#5/#6/#7/#9) compiling to verdict.sabra's exact hash and step count - the gate-receipts source itself is left to its chapter's owner to switch; corpus 98 -> 100 (dialect_tag-literal, dialect_cond), verify-differential 100/100; dune build @all and dune runtest exit 0. verify-12-dialect.sh exits 1 at 12.1(v0.2) on a STALE FIXTURE that predates this chapter: since dialect v0.3 (f899b547) keyed literals sort by key name, and the script's 4-field twin still lists key-who before key-when; a scratch copy with only that twin reordered passes all of 12.1-12.6 (ACCEPT 12 OK) on an isolated dev instance. Also seen: scripts/dialect/bridge_probe.py reads /tmp/tuna-dev/bootstrap.token and ignores TUNA_DEV_DIR, so on a machine with another dev instance it needs TUNA_SMOKE_TOKEN. Neither harness fix is made here (dialect chapter scope).|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Two forms, both total reader folds under dialect L2 and 12.1
      (form and hand twin compile to the identical tree and steps):

      COND. (cond (C1 A1) (C2 A2) ... (else E)) reads condition first
         and desugars to (if A1 (cond ...rest) C1), ending in E. The
         else clause is required (totality by construction, as match
         requires all three arms). cond leans on the stdlib name if,
         under the dialect letrec law: keywords may lean on stdlib
         names, resolution order stands, shadowing wins.
      TAG LITERALS. #5 reads as the law-4 small nat stem^5(leaf), the
         tree %111110. Digits only, no sign, #0 through #4096; #0 is the leaf. Tags are
         what records key on (dialect records: small-nat tags won the
         key-domain measurement), so a tag deserves a literal the way
         a law-5 int has one.

      Reservation audit as in 12.2: no stdlib name, corpus fixture or
      in-tree .sabra source uses cond as a form head or an atom
      starting with #. |))))
  (subsection t4-json-codec
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      A rim codec, host OCaml beside Tuna.Cstr, that maps JSON to the
      dialect record conventions and back: object -> record sorted by
      key (keys through a declared schema of name -> tag, never
      guessed), array -> list, string -> cstr, integer -> law-5 int,
      true/false -> bool, null -> none. The codec is total and
      round-trips on the documented subset; anything else (floats,
      unknown keys) is a 400 naming the field. A run endpoint accepts
      a JSON request plus a schema reference and answers the result
      decoded through the same schema, beside the raw result hash and
      run id. The calculus never sees JSON. |))))
  (subsection failure-definitions
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      TL1 COMBINATOR DISAGREEMENT. Any T1 source compiles to a hash
          other than the one agora published. THEN: record both trees
          in FINDINGS.md and decide which compiler revision is right
          before shipping the row.
      TL2 SUGAR SEAM. A T2 or T3 form needs an IR node kind or an
          interpreter change, or a form and its twin differ in tree
          or steps. THEN: drop the form (dialect F13 law).
      TL3 NO TAKER. T4 lands and no consumer outside tuna (erde,
          factory, saguaro) calls a sabra program through it within
          the chapter's make. THEN: the tool-call thesis is
          unproven; record it, and do not grow the language further
          on its account. |))))
 )
 (verify
  (test "T1: comb-i, comb-s, comb-b, comb-c and comb-w compile from their textbook sources to agora's published hashes 75bae64a, 6ab5ad37, e4b1abc3, a9d528d5 and a24e4471; the 76 earlier manifest rows are unchanged")
  (test "T1: corpus rows exercise each combinator's law (I x = x, S f g x = f x (g x), B f g x = f (g x), C f x y = f y x, W f x = f x x) and agree across engines")
  (test "T2: a pinned name with a matching prefix compiles to the same tree as the unpinned source; a wrong prefix is a compile error naming both hashes; a pin on a lambda parameter is an error")
  (test "T2: verdict-std.sabra (helpers deleted, stdlib names pinned) answers as verdict.sabra on every gate-receipt policy case, and its pins all hold in the lock /api/programs reports")
  (test "T3: each cond and #n form compiles to the identical tree and step count as its hand-written twin, and verdict.sabra rewritten with cond and #n compiles to the hash of the current verdict.sabra")
  (test "T4: a JSON request with a declared schema round-trips through the codec, and a run over it answers the decoded result plus the run id")))
